  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
